# THE DOCUMENT-TO-DOCUMENT REFERENCES A FILING ACTUALLY MAKES. One row per (filing, foreign
# notation) pair it reaches through a part.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THIS IS WHAT AN `import` IS, AND `pm.filing_identity` IS NOT IT. That table is every
#    document declaring its OWN uri: `asserted_by` reads *the filing, about itself*. In BPMN that
#    is `targetNamespace`, one per document. An `import` is a document naming ANOTHER one, and
#    nothing in the model files those directly: they are DERIVED from the references that cross a
#    document boundary.
#
# ⛔⛔⛔ AND THE PARTS ARE NOT THE ONLY PLACE THIS MODEL REACHES OUT OF A FILING. There are TWO
#    `pm:ForeignId` references: a `pm:Part`, and an `asrt:Elimination/between`. Both name a
#    notation and a layer in another document, and the second had no relation calling it a
#    reference, so every enumeration of what this model reaches returned the parts alone.
#    ⭐ In THIS corpus the union changes nothing, because all 3 documents a `between` names are
#    already reached by a part. That is luck and not construction, and a count cannot tell the
#    difference, which is why the union is taken here rather than argued about.
#
# ⛔ AN IDENTITY TABLE IS A `targetNamespace` AND NOT AN `import`, AND A COUNT SETTLES IT.
#   Declared |import| = |notation| the law expects 15 and the emitter writes 5, and 5 is right:
#   `asserted_by` reads the filing about ITSELF, where an import is a document naming another.
#   A law is worth having exactly when it can contradict the person who wrote it.
-- every reference that leaves its own document: diagrams/calls.sqlc's foreign parts, and the
-- filed layers an elimination is stated between.
SELECT DISTINCT x.composition, x.part_notation
FROM (
    SELECT c.composition, c.part_notation
    FROM ( :compose(diagrams/calls.sqlc) ) c
    WHERE NOT c.is_local
    UNION
    SELECT b.composition, b.notation
    FROM ( :compose(eliminations/references.sqlc) ) b
) x
